>(Mild) spoiler alert:
>
>There are good ways and bad ways to open a locked chest.
>(Of course, not all chests are locked.)  There are key
>that fits, skeleton key, lock pick, and credit card, with
>the only adverse effects coming if the chest is trapped.
>Kicking the chest will never (in my experience) destroy
>the chest, but can cost hit points and leave your leg (or
>hind limb, depending) in no shape to kick.  This also
>temporarily reduces your strength.  In addition, there's
>still the possibility of a trapped chest.
>
>Then there's the possibility of forcing the chest.  If you
>use an edged weapon, you will probably damage it.  (The
>edged weapon, that is.)  I have always been able to wield
>a pickaxe, mace, or statue and attempt to force the lock
>without damaging what was being wielded.  If you keep at
>it long enough, you will eventually force the lock but you
>may destroy the chest.  I suppose that's still of some
>importance.  It was critical back in the days of pl6 when
>you could still polymorph random tools into magic lamps.
>And a trapped chest is still a pain.
>
>So the best way seems to be to unlock it.  Failing that,
>you can kick it and do damage to your foot or force it and
>do damage to a weapon and/or the chest.  Some wielded
>objects are not damaged.
>
>-- Don Varvel (varvel@cs.utexas.edu)


